\import{fcap-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}

\tag{fcap}
\tag{spin}
\tag{clifford}

\taxon{Example}
\title{The split three-dimensional Spin group is #{SL_2(\mathbb R)}}

\p{For #{Q_{2,1}(x_0,x_1,x_2)=x_0^2+x_1^2-x_2^2}, set
#{A=e_0e_1}, #{B=e_0e_2}, and #{C=e_1e_2}. Then

##{A^2=-1,\qquad B^2=C^2=1,\qquad AB=-C.}

The assignments

##{A\longmapsto\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\-1&0\end{pmatrix},\qquad
B\longmapsto\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\1&0\end{pmatrix},\qquad
C\longmapsto\begin{pmatrix}-1&0\\0&1\end{pmatrix}}

satisfy these relations, and the images of #{1,A,B,C} form a basis of
#{M_2(\mathbb R)}. They therefore define an algebra equivalence #{\Phi} with

##{\Phi(s+aA+bB+cC)=
\begin{pmatrix}s-c&a+b\\-a+b&s+c\end{pmatrix}.}

Reversal negates #{A,B,C}, so it becomes matrix adjugation, and

##{N_{\mathrm{rev}}(s+aA+bB+cC)
=s^2+a^2-b^2-c^2=\det\Phi(s+aA+bB+cC)}

\leanref/tauceti{CliffordAlgebra.map_reverseEven_eq_adjugate_of_finrank_eq_three}
\leanref/tauceti{CliffordAlgebra.reverseEven_mul_eq_one_iff_det_eq_one_of_finrank_eq_three}.}

\p{Restriction gives #{\Spin(Q_{2,1})\to SL_2(\mathbb R)}. Conversely, if
#{M=(m_{ij})\in SL_2(\mathbb R)}, put

##{\begin{aligned}
s&=\frac{m_{00}+m_{11}}2,&
a&=\frac{m_{01}-m_{10}}2,\\
b&=\frac{m_{01}+m_{10}}2,&
c&=\frac{m_{11}-m_{00}}2.
\end{aligned}}

Then #{\Phi(s+aA+bB+cC)=M}, and its reverse norm is #{\det M=1}. The carrier
lemma puts this inverse value in the actual Spin group; the algebra maps give
two-sided multiplicative inverses. Thus

##{\Spin(Q_{2,1})\simeq SL_2(\mathbb R).}}

\p{The source translation matters. Lawson--Michelsohn's convention turns
this even algebra into #{\operatorname{Cl}_{0,2}\simeq M_2(\mathbb R)} via
\citet{I.3, Theorem 3.7, pp. 23--24; I.4, (4.0), p. 25}{lawson2016spin}.
With the reversed index convention,
\citet{Example 1.3, p. 33}{gallier2014clifford} independently records
#{\operatorname{Cl}_{2,0}\simeq M_2(\mathbb R)}, the same real matrix base
algebra. Their indefinite Pin/Spin carrier conventions allow factors of
quadratic value #{\pm1}
\citet{I.2, (2.24)--(2.25), p. 18}{lawson2016spin}
\citet{Section 1.5, Definition 1.6, p. 31}{gallier2014clifford}. We use their
algebra identifications but impose the displayed reverse-norm-one equation.
For example, #{e_0e_2} has reverse norm #{-1} and maps to a determinant
#{-1} matrix, so it is excluded here.}

\p{This split form has polarization
#{W=\mathbb R(e_0+e_2)},
#{W'=\mathbb R((e_0-e_2)/4)}, and line #{\mathbb R e_1}: the unhalved polar
pairing of the displayed generators is #{1}, and the line coordinate squares
to #{Q_{2,1}}. TauCeti's polarized dimension-three theorem therefore gives
an independent abstract group equivalence with #{SL_2(\mathbb R)}
\leanref/tauceti{TauCeti.exists_spinGroup_mulEquiv_specialLinearGroup_and_spinRep_equiv_stdSLRep_of_finrank_eq_three}.
The concrete map #{\Phi} above is not asserted to be definitionally that map,
and the theorem's representation refinement belongs to the later module
packet. Likewise, the vector-action image is the spinor-norm kernel from
\ref{fcap-0023}, not all of #{SO(2,1)}. No component or covering-space claim
is made.}
